{
  "evaluation": "All three appropriately classify the disagreement as genuine and prioritize design_or_bias: a randomized supplement trial versus an observational serum-vitamin-D cohort. None improperly treats the small trial as underpowered or assumes that different symptom measures necessarily dissolve the conflict. B is strongest because it accurately preserves the distinction between a supplementation benefit and a null association, and describes the duration as up to 180 days. C is nearly equivalent but slightly overstates the trial duration as 180 days. A is also sound, though its emphasis on sample size is less explanatory than B's emphasis on the exposure and follow-up. All three assume the proprietary supplement contains vitamin D, which the supplied fields do not explicitly confirm.",
  "ranking": ["B", "C", "A"]
}
